  "decision": {
    "answer": "REVISIONS",
    "explanation": "The provided evidence (pmid-27489350:f01) supports the claim that PIK3CA mutations confer cisplatin resistance in cervical cancer cells. However, the evidence specifically identifies the PIK3CA-E545K variant, not the PIK3CA-E406D variant specified in the claim. While both are hotspot mutations in PIK3CA, they are distinct variants. The evidence does not support the specific variant E406D. Therefore, the claim requires revision to reflect the correct variant (E545K) supported by the evidence.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-27489350",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 599,
        "quote": "The purpose of this study was to determine whether PIK3CA-E545K mutation renders cervical cancer cells more resistant to cisplatin and/or radiation, and whether PI3K inhibition reverses the phenotype. We found that CaSki cells that are heterozygous for the PIK3CA-E545K mutation are more resistant to cisplatin or cisplatin plus radiation than either HeLa or SiHa cells that express only wild-type PIK3CA. Similarly, HeLa cells engineered to stably express PIK3CA-E545K were more resistant to cisplatin or cisplatin plus radiation than cells expressing only wild-type PIK3CA or with PIK3CA depleted."
      }
    ],
